From c67a728c2259015ae128d51f0e34b1fac1562d39 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Rajan <7121943+kvnloo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 02:39:37 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] fix(desktop): keep a stale no-owner recovery from latching a
 live session read-only

A no-owner recovery (#94724) awaits the owner probe and a cross-backend
stored read before it opens the stored transcript read-only. If a live
resume of the same stored id lands in the meantime (the owner backfill
stamped the row), the stale recovery still finished last and:

- set the global read-only latch over the live outcome, because the live
  resume's clear ran while the latch was not set yet, so main-pane sends
  for that session were refused; and
- in a tile, repainted it read-only and repointed the stored -> runtime
  binding onto the synthetic read-only:<id>, so the warm path kept reusing
  it and the tile refused sends for good.

The main pane already drops its own superseded resumes with
isCurrentResume(); the helper-backed tile path had no analog, and neither
path noticed a live resume of the same id made by the other.

- read-only-transcript: every successful live resume bumps a per-stored-id
  generation, even when the clear is a no-op, read via
  liveResumeGeneration(). resumeWithStoredTranscriptFallback latches only
  if it has not moved since the attempt started (optional snapshot
  argument, defaulting to call time).
- use-session-tile-delegate: resumeTile snapshots the generation on entry
  and passes it to the helper; once a live resume has bound the session, a
  read-only outcome returns that binding instead of painting.
- use-session-actions: resumeSession snapshots it on entry; the main-pane
  no-owner recovery latches and toasts only if it has not moved.

